==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|la|piscātor||fisherman}}, from {{m|la|piscis||fish}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|en|ɔːɹiəl|s=5}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Of or pertaining to [[fishermen]] or [[fishing]].
#* {{RQ:Trollope The Claverings|chapter=41|passage=There should be no plea put in by him in his absences, that he had only gone to catch a few fish, when his intentions had been other than '''piscatorial'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1895|journal=The Gentleman's Magazine|section=January to June issue, [http://books.google.com/books?id=qTDNv_biFioC&pg=PA38&dq=%22a+lucy%22+date:1800-1900+pike&lr= pg. 38]
|passage=That a lucy or luce is the mature pike, every '''piscatorial''' schoolboy knows.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=Jane lined up the girls and called out, "Dive!" The girls dived - mermaids for a '''piscatorial''' pageant.|page=184|title=Vice in Bombay|author=Allen V. Ross|publisher=Tallis Press|location=London|year=1969}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Tom Fort|title=Trout fishing in Chilean Patagonia [print version: Gone fishing&nbsp;– and waking up in heaven, 4 April 2015, p.&nbsp;T6]|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/travel/destinations/southamerica/chile/11511603/Trout-fishing-in-Chilean-Patagonia.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150406150655/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/travel/destinations/southamerica/chile/11511603/Trout-fishing-in-Chilean-Patagonia.html|archivedate=6 April 2015|work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Travel)|date=2 April 2015|passage=The theme is unashamedly '''piscatorial'''&nbsp;– there are models of trout, pictures of trout, books about trout, a cabinet full of fly-tying gear. The veranda, cluttered with waders, boots, rods and tackle, is where the fishing day begins.}}
# Of or pertaining to [[fish]]; [[piscine]].
